# voice-rules.md

Canonical hard rules for writing under Brian Moriarty's name. Single source of truth. Every other voice file's ban list is derived from this one; do not maintain a second copy. Merged from the Voice Markers Reference, the anti-slop instructions, the Voice Guide, Voice File v2, VOICE_AND_FIREWALL, project memory, and mined session corrections. Conflicts resolved per the five rulings recorded at the end.

Four buckets: phrase bans, structural tells, mechanical bans, context-specific bans. A single appearance in the right context can be correct; the tell is reflex and repetition. Density rule applies throughout: more than two flagged constructions per 100 words is a warning even when no single hit forces a revision.

---

## Bucket 1: Hard phrase bans

The organizing test, in Brian's words: most of the words he dislikes sound like product marketing. If a word would be at home in a product launch, a sales deck, or a marketing landing page, cut it. That one test catches most of the list below. The list is the worked-out consequence, not a set to memorize. The word itself is rarely the problem; the pitch register is.

Banned as filler, intensifier, or connective tissue. Where a substitution is known, it follows.

### AI filler and inflated vocabulary (the union of every list)

significant, crucial, essential, important, key (as adjective), vital, pivotal, meaningful, robust, holistic, intricate, nuanced, valuable, leverage (as verb; use "use"), utilize, delve / delves / delving, tapestry, showcases, underscores (as verb), navigating (as metaphor), emphasizing, enhance, highlighting, showcasing, aligns with, fostering, landscape (as abstract noun), in the realm of, essentially, fundamentally, ultimately (when hedging, not when sequencing), in many ways, a testament to, speaks to, seamless / seamlessly, unlock (as verb), synergy / synergistic, impactful, journey (for a program or transition), best-in-class, world-class, cutting-edge, transformative, revolutionary, game-changer, paradigm shift, "it's worth noting" / "it is worth noting" / "it's important to note."

**Persistent crutch, scan for it first: "important."** It is banned above, and it is the word Brian reaches for most: the AISHK-endorsement placeholder plus two more times in the same short draft (2026-07-19). Treat it as the first thing to check in any draft, not just one entry in the list. Preferred replacements depend on the sentence: "serious," "worth doing," "worth backing," or cut it and let the specifics carry the weight.

### Hype and boosters

incredibly, truly, really, very, extremely, thrilled to, excited to, passionate about, "real work" / "doing real work."

### Authenticity-signaling words (tell when used to sound human)

genuinely, honestly, truthfully, frankly, candidly, "to be honest," "in all honesty," "I'll be real with you," "I'll be straight with you," "owning it." This includes the mid-sentence filler use, for example "watch its tier rise, honestly"; cut it or restructure.

### Personal bans with substitutions (from your files and mined corrections)

- "genuinely / genuine" → "substantive"
- "shape" (as filler verb or noun), "co-actor" → name the specific action
- "sounds like a good fit" / "exactly the right move" → "right up my alley"
- "Here's the catch" → "The problem is"
- "quiet" / "going quiet" meaning someone went silent → "dropped off the radar" (this is the noun/silence sense; the adverb "quietly" is handled in Bucket 2)
- "silently" as an insinuating adverb ("silently upgraded," "silently dropped") → "without notice," or restructure. Same insinuating-adverb family as trailing "quietly" (Bucket 2, RULING C2)
- "I'll turn it around" → "I'll review and work on it"
- "Here's the short version" → "Here's the quick version"
- "a review happened" → "a review occurred"
- metaphorical "land" / "lands" / "landing" ("would land easier," "work landing in the repo," "didn't land with the team") → "would be easier," "would be preferable," "committed in the repo," "resonate," "make sense to," "sound good"
- "responsible for" (career writing) → what was built, directed, designed, founded, delivered
- overused "on paper" and "decreased" → vary; keep only where a template forces it
- "Good to hear" → "Glad to hear"; prefer "Glad"
- "a read from the group" → "feedback from the group"
- "on my end" / "on my end for now" → "on my side" / "on my side at the moment"
- figurative phrasing → the plain, common word. Prefer plain over figurative throughout
- "significant improvement" → "marked improvement"
- "leverage [X]" → "take advantage of [X]"
- tool value: prefer plain "worth the money" over "earns its keep"
- "pays for itself" -> state the benefit plainly: what you get back, and roughly when. Do not use the idiom.
- "worth its weight" (in gold or otherwise) -> say plainly what it is worth, or drop it
- deployment, turning a system on: "going live in production," not "stood up" or "deployed"
- abstract emotional framing → the concrete result. "losing my own voice" → "getting results that didn't sound like me" (2026-07-21)
- vague pointer phrase "the thing that pushed me" / "the thing that [X]" → "what inspired me," or name the cause plainly (2026-07-21)
- capability-contrast "does the part a regex cannot" → "complements the regex" (see Bucket 2; 2026-07-21)
- "terrain" (figurative, for someone's area) → "domain of expertise," name the field plainly (2026-08)
- "react to" → "respond to" (2026-08)
- internal or deployment slang in reader-facing material, for example "the box" (our server), "spun up," "stood up" → name it plainly ("live," "running now," "the server"), or drop it. Flagged on the platform one-pager (2026-08-21)
- do not stamp the audience on an artifact, for example a masthead meta line "for the core faculty" → drop it; the audience is who you send it to, not a label on the page (2026-08-21)

### The test

If a sentence needs one of these to work, the sentence is wrong. Rewrite with specific, observational, or operational language.

