## Bucket 2: Structural tells

### Antithesis family (single instance can be your voice; two or more in a paragraph is the tell)

"not X, but Y"; "not just X, it's Y"; "it doesn't just X, it Y"; "this is not X, it is Y"; "X isn't just Y, it's Z"; "not merely X but Y." Includes negative parallelisms dressed as insight.

### Triplet noun piling (single ok; two or more per paragraph is the tell)

Three abstract nouns coordinated where one or two serve ("clarity, transparency, and accountability"). Also the rule of three in adjectives or short phrases chained for rhythm.

### Copulative avoidance

Use "is" and "are." Do not replace with "serves as," "stands as," "represents," "marks," "features," "boasts," "offers."

### Elegant variation

Do not swap synonyms to avoid repeating a name or term. Second reference to Claude is "Claude," not "the assistant" or "the tool."

### Scene-setting and hedge openers

"In a world where," "In the rapidly evolving landscape of," "As we navigate," "Today, more than ever," "In recent years," "Picture this:," "Imagine [scenario]," "Situated [phrase], the [noun]..."

### Engagement-bait transitions

"But here's the part no one is talking about," "Here's what they don't tell you," "And that's the thing," "Here's the real question," "The truth is," "Let me tell you why," "And here's why that matters," "Plot twist:."

### Paragraph-opener connectives

"Furthermore," "Moreover," "Additionally," "In conclusion," "To summarize," "In summary," "Overall," "Ultimately."

### Portentous transitions and forward pointers (personal)

"Which is where the real question sits," "the one almost no one is asking," "I keep returning to that," "the next thing worth looking at," "the move that starts many of these," "worth about ten minutes," "the thing worth knowing is," "what X looks like" nominalizations.

### The watching construction (personal, kill on sight)

"I have watched," "watched enough of these go in," "one tool go into a place." De-identify and vary instead.

### Interrogative headers (2026-08-23)

A heading that opens **What, Where, Why, How, When, Which or Who** is a question
fragment standing in where a name should be: "What the prompt decides", "Where
tonight sits", "What multi-agent costs". The heading asks something and the
section immediately answers it, so the question does no work; it is a throat-clear
with a capital letter.

**This is a rate, not a rule.** One or two in a document is often the clearest
option and a genuine question ("Which one was yours?", ending in a question mark)
is correct usage. The tell is density: a document where a fifth or more of the
headings do it has stopped naming its sections and started interviewing itself.

Replace with a noun phrase or an imperative, which is what the heading was for:
"Six parts of an analytical prompt", "The cost of coordination", "Define success
first", "Check the seams".

**Decks and prose need different thresholds, and the difference is real.** A deck
is its titles, so the rate separates cleanly: measured across fourteen on
2026-08-23, decks that read well sat at 4-16% and ones flagged as AI voice sat at
20-25%, with a clean gap between. Prose headings are navigation over text that
carries its own meaning, so "What Week 5 Covered" is fine and document conventions
lift every file. Across 137 prose documents the distribution is smooth with no
gap: median 12.5%, tail to 50%.

So `structlint` flags prose only at 30% with ten or more headings, which catches
the tail without fighting house conventions. Slide decks want a stricter setting;
FA550 uses a dedicated tool at 18%.

### Cheeseball aphorism

A clipped line whose only job is to sound wise ("Knowing did not move it"). Cut it or fold it into the surrounding prose. This includes the balanced "X is A, Y is B" one-liner ("Your instinct was right, the measuring told us which structure"): the neat parallel is the tell. Flag it even as a single instance, unlike the antithesis family which needs two or more.

**The move outlives any phrase that carries it (2026-08-21).** Brian's observation:
as figurative "load-bearing" left the output, "is worth more than" arrived to do the
same job. Current vehicles are the comparative-worth family, "X is worth more than
Y," "is what separates X from Y," "counts for more than." Expect the list to be
incomplete, because banning a token relocates the habit instead of removing it. The
reliable check is functional rather than lexical: if a closing sentence asserts the
relative worth of two abstractions, and the paragraph stands without it, cut it. The
reflex to give a paragraph a final beat is the thing to refuse; the phrasing is only
where it happens to land this month.

### Invented specificity for cadence (personal, 2026-07-21)

An incidental precise detail supplied for rhythm or verisimilitude when it is not true or not known: a time-beat ("a day later"), a round count ("271 em dashes"). Distinct from the telling-wrong-detail archetype in Voice_Profile.md, which is powerful precisely because it is real. If the detail did not happen or is not known, cut it and understate. Reinforces the Factual-accuracy guard.

### Capability-contrast construction (personal, 2026-07-21)

The tidy "X does the part Y cannot," "does what Y can't," "the part a regex cannot reach." A capability antithesis dressed as insight. Replace with a plain verb: "complements," "works with," "adds what Y misses." Related to the antithesis family above.

### Present-participle significance tails

"...contributing to the broader history of," "...reflecting the enduring spirit of," "...highlighting its role as."

### Significance inflation

Do not describe routine facts as pivotal moments or turning points.

### Outline-style and summary closers

"Despite its challenges, X continues to thrive," "The future of X lies in," a "Challenges and Future Directions" section not called for by the source, summary paragraphs that restate the introduction, "In conclusion," "To summarize."

### Counter-X constructions

"counter-" prefixed to an abstract noun where a plain word serves; "counter-intuitive" (hyphenated form); "a [noun] of [hyphenated abstract]."

### Hedge-stacking and balanced-nothing sentences

Piling "may," "potentially," "somewhat" into one sentence. "While X is important, Y is equally critical."

### Compound-hyphen abstractions

"better-serving-the-question." Expand into a clause.

### Harsh rhetorical dismissals

"useless," "dumb," flat "bad" with no specifics. Critique by naming the specific failure.

### Reflexive praise

No hollow compliment openers ("Good work getting X already"). Convert praise into a curiosity question, or cut it.

### Register drop (personal)

A slang or throwaway beat that undercuts an otherwise controlled piece ("the raise that feels kinda meh"). In a measured op-ed, paper, or brief, cut the slang or lift it to the register around it. Casual chat and email are exempt; this is about tonal consistency within a controlled surface.

### Slide closers (2026-08-06)

On a slide, a trailing italic one-liner under the bullets is usually a cheeseball closer that exists only to sound wise; cut it. A plain directive or instruction as a closer is fine ("build carefully," "run it on Mentimeter"). The tell is the wise-sounding aphorism, not the closing position. Related to Cheeseball aphorism above.

### Empty-emphasis frame (2026-08-06)

The frame that promises weight and never delivers a verb: "the one(s) that ...," "is the thing that ...," "are the ones that ...." Use the direct verb. "The measuring told us which structure," not "measuring is the thing that told us." Judgment rule; the linter over-fires on the bare strings.

### Dramatic stance headers (2026-08-06)

A header that strikes a pose instead of naming its subject: "The one thing that sinks it," "Why this matters," "The part everyone misses." Headers name the subject plainly: "Failure modes," "The masthead," "What the footnote is for." Same product-launch tell as body hype, in title case.

### Comma-series fragment as a sentence (2026-08-06)

A comma series standing in for a sentence: "A claim, the experiment, and a baseline." It reads like a slide header and detaches the list from any action. Write a clause: "Each entry pairs a claim with the experiment that tests it and a baseline to beat." Same family as First person and active over telegraphic fragments in voice-authoring.md.

### Compressed pronoun antithesis (2026-08)

The punchy "X; it didn't Y" with a semicolon and a bare pronoun reads as manufactured cadence: "AI distorts it; it didn't invent it." Spell out the noun and join with "but": "AI distorts it, but it didn't invent the surrender scenario." Same family as the antithesis family above and the specific-noun move in voice-authoring.md.

### Staccato runs (RULING C1)

A single emphatic short sentence after a longer one is correct and is your voice. A run of consecutive short sentences is the tell; merge them. Be selective: reserve the short sentence for emphasis only.

### Trailing "quietly" (RULING C2, positional)

Flag "quietly" only when it ends a clause or sentence, the insinuating position that implies concealed intent ("shut it down quietly," "the numbers moved quietly"). Pre-modifier stance is your voice and is not flagged ("quietly skeptical," "quietly noticing"). This is the rule shipped in Pherkad; it supersedes the older outright ban.

### Crutch overuse, density-weighted

"exactly," "actually," "precisely." Keep the one load-bearing use; cut the rest and count them toward density.

### Absolutes

Avoid "no" and "never" as blanket claims. Prefer "rarely," "seldom," "almost never," "few." Hedge structurally, not defensively.

