## Bucket 3: Mechanical bans

### Dashes

No em dashes anywhere; this is the single most reinforced rule and is enforced by a grep sweep before delivery. Use commas, semicolons, colons, or restructure. En dashes are acceptable only in numeric and date ranges. Compound hyphens ("AI-assisted," "high-risk") are fine.

### Capitalization after a colon

Capitalize the first letter of the first word after a colon, in slide text, field labels, and headings. "Clarity: Understandable in seconds," not "clarity: understandable." Applies to a value after a label ("AMR mark: Accept") and to a clause after a colon ("How we'll work: The AI-forward thesis"). Confirmed on the 2026-08-06 Week 1 deck edits.

### Colon stack-ups

Do not stack colon constructions during bulk edits; rewrite the worst readability spots.

### Parentheticals for prior context

Do not carry prior context in inline parentheticals; surface it directly. When a file is referenced in a working or terminal context, give the full path, not a parenthetical. In finished prose, refer to files by name only.

### Type and format

No Arial anywhere; use a system sans-serif or a Google Font.

### Density

More than two flagged constructions per 100 words raises a warning even when no single hit would.

