## Bucket 4: Context-specific bans

### First person, by surface (RULING C3)

| Surface | First person |
|---------|--------------|
| Research papers | Royal "we"; no first-person singular, including the introduction |
| Blog posts | First person allowed |
| Op-eds | First person allowed |
| Email | First person, warm and direct |

### Email register

More friendly and colloquial, less email-formal. Contractions, casual openers. After any back-and-forth, make it exceptionally short. Standing live note: shorten.

Salutation punctuation is loose: "Hi Bernard." with a period, "Hi Pavel" with nothing, and "Hi [Name]," with a comma all occur. Do not enforce one; leave it as written. Sign-off is "Best, Brian" for faculty and external contacts; the terse "-Brian" is the internal or casual form. Cut routine logistical gratitude: do not thank someone for a routine step, go straight to the forward-looking line.

Moves from the 2026-07-20 faculty-reply batch (his edits on four near-identical drafts):

- Keep the concrete tech noun. "UI and backend," not a smoothed generic like "the platform"; he reverts that generalization every time.
- Defuse dangling paperwork with a warm, plain reassurance ("don't worry it's not too much," "nothing too formal") rather than leaving "release form" or "the survey" hanging as an abstract object, which reads as ominous. Name what it is, lightly, or defer it as "what's involved."
- Tailor the closer to the person: "Glad you're able to assist" for someone who offered to assist, not a generic "Glad you're in."

Moves from the 2026-08 Ho-Dac intro-request rewrite (writing to a known or senior contact):

- Acknowledge what the recipient already shares with him; do not explain as if cold. When the reader has prior involvement, name it: "As you know since you helped me conceptualize it, my planned brief argues ...," and "As I think I shared, my thinking behind the brief didn't start with AI," not a cold "My brief argues for ...."
- Hedge claims about other people's expertise, and admit his own uncertainty plainly: "From my brief read, that's Marion's domain of expertise," not "That's Ho-Dac's terrain"; "Two things she'd help me with, given that I am a little uncertain in these areas," not a flat "Two things she'd help me with."
- First name for a mutual contact. Writing to someone who knows the person, use the first name ("Marion"), not the surname ("Ho-Dac").
- Courteous register in an ask to a senior or external contact: "which is part of why I would appreciate the introduction," not "which is part of why I want the intro."
- Do not let a set of similar emails share one skeleton (apology, then process line, then a warm button). Parallel structure across recipients reads as canned even when the words differ; vary shape and length per message.
- Prefer concrete active phrasing over vague coverage: "before we put them to use," not "before anything goes further with them."

Moves from the 2026-08-01 team security-update email (his edits on the draft):

- Group greeting: open with "Hi everyone" or "Hi all," never "Hi team" (he dislikes it). Single named recipient still takes "Hi [Name]".
- First person for what he did; "we" only for the shared ask. "I ran two passes," "I found seven issues," "I set up" for his own work; "we should set up," "we should confirm," "we should hold" for the collective, forward-looking asks. Do not fold his solo work into a collective "we."
- Link issues and pull requests as markdown links (`[#86](url)`), not bare "#86."

### Practitioner versus academic register (novelty firewall)

Papers may use the full research vocabulary. Practitioner surfaces (Substack, SREcon, GitHub Universe, LinkedIn) stay at the symptom and observation level. Reserved vocabulary never appears on practitioner surfaces. Details live in the private firewall file, not here.

### Policy and lay-jury audiences

Flatten jargon into plain, connectable language. "Is the scaling case something people can connect to," not "is the scaling case tight enough for the jury."

### Documents meant to be read (agendas, briefs)

Much shorter, scannable in under a minute. Lead with the recommendation. Answer the question actually asked. Translate jargon or skip it. Lead with the institution name, not an internal acronym.

### Student communications

Plain, direct, warm but not promotional. Stepwise for procedures. Assume limited command-line familiarity.

### Career writing (resume, cover letter, LinkedIn)

No "responsible for"; open bullets with a strong specific verb (built, directed, founded, designed, converted, delivered). Precise numbers when known, honestly approximate ("roughly 300") when not; never fake precision. No hype. Program over "project" for multi-year or cross-team work.

### Factual-accuracy guard

Do not credit Brian with coining terms he only researches. Do not fabricate plausible-but-false specifics; say "no course moves this" rather than invent a study target. This includes numbers and time-beats supplied for effect ("271," "a day later") in casual prose, not only formal claims: understate rather than embellish (see Bucket 2, invented specificity).

