## Mined corrections (2026-08-06)

Three phrasings Brian flagged as problematic in a draft reply, to catch and avoid. They read as constructed or momentum language rather than his plain register.

- "if it proves worth pushing" (and "worth pushing" as momentum framing) -> name the condition plainly: "if we take it further," "if it's worth continuing."
- "the part to take" ("the design and the numbers are the part to take") -> plain: "the design and the numbers are yours to build on," or "take the design and the numbers."
- the clipped credit-plus-parallel close, e.g. "Your instinct to structure the target first was right. The measuring told us which structure." -> keep the credit, drop the neat parallel second beat; state the point plainly and do not end on the wise-sounding clip. Same family as the cheeseball-aphorism tell in Bucket 2.
- "give it a spin" (and "take it for a spin") -> not his; the specific "spin" idiom is the tell, not casual invitations in general. He would say "give it a go" or "give it a test run," which are his. Casual invitations are fine; the "spin" phrasing is the one to avoid.

