## Mined corrections (2026-08-14, Steve update email and deck)

Phrasings Brian replaced in a Steve-facing email and deck. Apply the right side. He does not want the left side either in his voice or as on-screen statements from Claude to him.

- "one honest note" (and framing something as an "honest note") -> "one observation". The "honest" framing is authenticity-signaling (Bucket 1); state the observation.
- Headers that pose a definition rather than name it: `What Counts as Working`, `What Makes a Prompt Analytical`. The subject **is** the definition, so the header can be the term. Enforced in `structlint` alongside `Where X sits`.

- The whole `[det] honest [adj]NOUN` family, not the twelve `the honest X` literals it used to be. `One Honest First Look` shipped into a course document on 2026-08-25 because the ban began with "the". The nouns are the ones that name the utterance (answer, version, truth, read, take, look, note, framing, thing, part, case, move, position, limit, summary), not the ones that name a real object. `honest broker`, `honest axis`, `honest accounting` and `honest assessment` are subject matter and do not fire.
- "where things landed" -> "where I was able to complete". Avoid the vague momentum idiom; say what was completed.
- "plainly" as a candor tag ("reports it plainly," "state plainly") -> lead with "Here is the reality," or just state it. Do not lean on "plainly" to carry candor. (Distinct from the positive "name it plainly" in voice-authoring; the tell is "plainly" as filler.)
- "reusable surface" (for the thing being built) -> "reusable instrument".
- "adding a course is one script" / overstating automation -> "one mostly automated script". Do not claim full automation.
- "connections worth a look" / "worth exploring" -> "connections worth reviewing".
- "I would pose these as" -> "I would suggest these as".
- Strike self-justifying trailing clauses such as "but that is the kind of thing X is for." Cut the justification and let the point stand.

